					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/captain-cain" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1522769); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1522769&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608930954" alt="Captain Cain" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1522769)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/captain-cain" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1522769); return false;" >Captain Cain</a></span> <span class="odate time_1378423149 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">05 Sep 2013 23:19</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1846428">
									

<blockquote>
<p>The thoroughness of the damage showed that the creature must have intelligently, methodically worked its way through the village, allowing nothing to escape destruction.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>These blast points&#8230; too accurate for Sand People. Only Imperial stormtroopers are so precise.</em></p>
<p>I liked this a lot. I know people say, if you do a cliche or something overdone (682, 173), you gotta do it good. This is quality writing, mate.</p>
<p>anqxyr: I, for one, was looking <em>forward</em> to the &quot;You are disgusting line&quot;, which sort of let me down.</p>
<p>Made three edits, for technical stuff, like repeated words and misspellings.</p>
<p>The Plague Doctor seems appropriate, but since 173 is concrete and Rebar, I figure somebody <em>made</em> hime, so I was surprised&#8230; Not to say it wasn't neat, but it threw me, and added an element of 'If he's been around this long, then WTF is he?'</p>

							</div>

								
					
